Title: Fall back to SwiftShader when no GL backend initializes at all
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

GpuInit has a SwiftShader fallback already, but it only covers a GPU that came
up and was then blocklisted -- it has to collect GL strings to make that
decision, so it needs a context first. The case where no display type
initializes at all has no fallback: InitializeGLNoExtensionsOneOff returns null
and InitializeAndStartSandbox returns false.

That exits the GPU process, viz launches a replacement, the replacement fails
identically, and nothing ever decides to stop and use software instead. On XP it
is reached by the shortest possible route -- D3D11 cannot start because there is
no DXGI, and D3D9 cannot start on a machine with no Direct3D display driver
(VirtualBox has not shipped one for pre-Vista guests since 5.x) -- so the
browser spins until it is killed, with no window. A Windows machine with no
usable driver at all gets there too.

So retry once with SwiftShader, which needs no driver. It is skipped when the
implementation was named with --use-gl or --use-angle, because someone who asked
for a specific backend should be told it failed rather than quietly handed
another one, and skipped when --disable-software-rasterizer says not to.

The retry has to put the implementation back to kGLImplementationNone before
re-initializing, since InitializeStaticGLBindingsOneOff DCHECKs that nothing is
set yet. ShutdownGL does that, and tolerates the null display -- there is no
display to tear down, which is the whole problem.

Not XP-specific, and worth sending upstream: the shape of the bug is an
unbounded process-restart loop on any host where GL cannot start.

diff --git a/gpu/ipc/service/gpu_init.cc b/gpu/ipc/service/gpu_init.cc
index b353b10839..0fd50cb5c4 100644
--- a/gpu/ipc/service/gpu_init.cc
+++ b/gpu/ipc/service/gpu_init.cc
@@ -511,7 +512,46 @@ bool GpuInit::InitializeAndStartSandbox(base::CommandLine* command_line,
         /*init_bindings*/ false, gl::GpuPreference::kDefault);
     if (!gl_display) {
       VLOG(1) << "gl::init::InitializeGLNoExtensionsOneOff failed";
-      return false;
+#if BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_SWIFTSHADER)
+      // No display type initialized at all. This is a different situation from
+      // the blocklisted-GPU fallback further down, which needs a GL context to
+      // have come up before it can ask the driver what it is: here the machine
+      // has no usable driver for any of the backends that were tried. Returning
+      // false exits the GPU process, viz launches a replacement, and that one
+      // fails in exactly the same way, so without a retry the process loops
+      // forever and the host never falls back to anything. Try SwiftShader
+      // once, which needs no driver at all.
+      //
+      // Deliberately skipped when the implementation was named on the command
+      // line: somebody who passed --use-gl or --use-angle wants to be told that
+      // the thing they asked for failed, not quietly handed something else.
+      if (!gl_use_swiftshader_ &&
+          !gpu_preferences_.disable_software_rasterizer &&
+          !gl::GetRequestedGLImplementationFromCommandLine(command_line)
+               .has_value()) {
+        command_line->AppendSwitchASCII(switches::kUseGL,
+                                        gl::kGLImplementationANGLEName);
+        command_line->AppendSwitchASCII(
+            switches::kUseANGLE, gl::kANGLEImplementationSwiftShaderName);
+        gl_use_swiftshader_ = true;
+
+        // Puts the implementation back to kGLImplementationNone so the
+        // bindings can be set up again for the new one -- initializing them
+        // twice is a DCHECK. A null display is expected and handled; there is
+        // no display to tear down because nothing initialized.
+        gl::init::ShutdownGL(/*display=*/nullptr, /*due_to_fallback=*/true);
+
+        gl_display = gl::init::InitializeGLNoExtensionsOneOff(
+            /*init_bindings=*/true, gl::GpuPreference::kDefault);
+        if (!gl_display) {
+          VLOG(1) << "gl::init::InitializeGLNoExtensionsOneOff with "
+                  << "SwiftShader failed";
+        }
+      }
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_SWIFTSHADER)
+      if (!gl_display) {
+        return false;
+      }
     }
   }
 
